course: dictionary
lesson: 7016
title: Parkland: open green land
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Parkland is a noun made from two words, park and land. It means open land covered with grass and trees, used as a park. Context helps: «We had a picnic on the parkland by the river, under the big oak trees.» Picnic, grass and trees are the clues, so parkland must be a green, open place.
explain: Parkland can also mean grassy land with scattered trees around a big country house. «Deer grazed on the parkland behind the old mansion.» Scattered means spread out here and there, not packed close together. The house is the clue that this is a grand, country kind of parkland.
explain: In towns, parkland describes green spaces for walking and play. Some places give it a name: in Adelaide, Australia, «the parklands» is the green belt around the city. When you see the word on a map, look at the words near it and ask: is this a green, open space?
fact: Parkland means open land with grass and trees, used as a park.
fact: «Parkland» is a noun, and its plural is «parklands».
fact: Grassy parkland can have scattered trees around a big country house.
fact: In Adelaide, Australia, «the parklands» is the green belt around the city.
fact: Context check: swap in a word like «park» or «grassy open space» and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «The children raced across the parkland to reach the swings.» What is the parkland here? | A school hall with a stage | *Open grassy land with trees, used as a park | A busy road with fast cars | A rocky mountain with no plants
exercise: «Deer grazed on the parkland, between the scattered oak trees.» What does scattered mean? | Painted bright green | *Spread out here and there | Packed tightly together | Cut down and removed
exercise: On a map of Adelaide, Australia, «the parklands» means | *The green belt around the city | A zoo with lots of animals | The airport for planes | The harbour where the boats are kept
test: What is the plural of parkland? | parkly | parkland | *parklands | parklanding
test: «We walked across the parkland to the river.» Which word could swap in for parkland and keep the meaning? | *park | road | river | cave
exam: «The house stood at the top of a hill, with parkland sloping down to the lake.» What does parkland most likely mean here? | *Grassy open land with scattered trees around a big house | A lake with boats on it | A dense, dark forest | A busy car park full of cars
